.TH ABI\-ENCODE 1 2026-10-25 "blocktools 0.1.1" "blocktools manual"
.SH NAME
abi\-encode \- Solidity ABI\-encode calldata (selector + args) or a bare type list, offline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
abi\-encode [SIGNATURE ARG...] [\-\-raw]
SIGNATURE 'name(T,..)' prepends the 4\-byte selector; '(T,..)' encodes the bare arguments.
An ARG starting with [ is parsed as JSON (arrays, tuples). With no SIGNATURE, reads {"sig":..,"args":[..]} from stdin.
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Arrays/tuples are JSON arrays; integers decimal or 0x\-hex; bytes 0x\-hex. Inverse of abi\-decode.
.nf
.RS 2
  abi\-encode 'transfer(address,uint256)' 0x5aAeb6053F3E94C9b9A09f33669435E7Ef1BeAed 1000000
  abi\-encode '(address,address,uint24)' 0x..a 0x..b 500 \-\-raw
  echo '{"sig":"f(uint256[])","args":[["1","2"]]}' | abi\-encode
.RE
.fi
.SH OPTIONS
.nf
  \-\-raw   print only the 0x\-hex data, for piping
.fi
.SH INPUT/OUTPUT
Arrays/tuples are JSON arrays; integers decimal or 0x\-hex; bytes 0x\-hex. Inverse of abi\-decode.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Measured on Linux by the EXAMPLES below (scripts/gen\-man.py runs them):
.TP
0
last status ok
.TP
2
last status bad_input
.PP
Not every tool uses the same code for bad input: some exit 1, others exit 2 (docs/FINDINGS.md D1\-14). Always read the last JSON status line. Status and failure codes: docs/REFUSALS.md.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
$ abi\-encode 'baz(uint32,bool)' 69 true
# exit 0, last status: ok
$ abi\-encode 'f(uint8)' 256
# exit 2, last status: bad_input
.fi
.SH SECURITY NOTES
DANGEROUS: use at your own risk. Code that handles keys and signs transactions should always be read and audited by you before you trust it with real funds. Used incorrectly, it can destroy your money.
Used incorrectly (wrong address, huge fee, lost keys, wrong chain) you lose funds; verify every transaction.
It may open hostile wallet files and handle secrets: run it sealed and offline (./blocktools run; sealed\-run allows it).
Unit tests are evidence, not a substitute for your own review. See docs/SECURITY.md.
